<TopicPage slug="subjects/surgical-technology/foundational/asepsis-and-the-sterile-field">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Surgical technology exists to protect the surgical wound from the microbial world. Its
    foundation is asepsis: creating a sterile field (instruments, drapes, gowns, gloves) and then
    defending it absolutely for the length of the case. The discipline's signature habit —
    <em>when in doubt, throw it out</em> — is what "sterile" actually means in practice.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from autoclave to guarded field" widget="equationStep" config={fieldCfg} />

  <h2>Sterilisation is verifiable</h2>
  <p>
    Steam under pressure remains the gold standard — and sterility is demonstrated, not assumed:
    chemical indicators on every pack, biological indicators (spore tests) regularly, and integrators
    inside each tray. Low-temperature methods (hydrogen peroxide plasma, ethylene oxide) cover
    cameras and cables that steam would destroy. The scrub tech prepares and checks all of it
    before a single incision.
  </p>

  <h2>The field has geography</h2>
  <p>
    Sterility is spatial: the draped table top and the front of a gowned person from chest to
    sterile-gloved hands are sterile; table edges, backs, below the waist, and anything out of
    sight are not. These boundaries — memorised as reflexes — are why scrubbed people pass
    back-to-back and face-to-face, and why a dropped instrument is never picked up.
  </p>

  <h2>Microbiology makes the rules</h2>
  <p>
    The stakes come from the organisms: skin flora (Staphylococcus), airborne spores, and the
    patient's own open bowel. Every aseptic rule maps to a transmission route — which is why
    surgical site infection rates are the measure of whether a team's asepsis is real, and why the
    tech's vigilance is patient safety, not ritual.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NBSTSA CST content outline; AST, <em>Surgical Technology for the Surgical Technologist</em>; AORN sterile technique guidelines.</p>
</TopicPage>
